//
// Expected: a0a1 at one session, a1a1 at two. ASSERT compares two terms for
// equality and nothing else, so what protects a value is not the comparison
// but the `?` that makes a failed comparison halt the principal. Bob runs two
// of them over four-deep hash chains: one checked, one not.
//
// The unchecked comparison fails on every honest run — shallow is not the deep
// chain and never was — and the model still loads, because an unchecked
// primitive that does not reduce is not an error. Bob simply carries on and
// uses the value anyway, which is exactly what makes the second query fail.
// core_rule_assert has to walk both terms to their leaves to answer the first
// one, so this is also the deepest equality comparison in the tree.
//
// At two sessions deep fails as well, for a reason the check has nothing to
// do with: deep is built from knows constants, so it is the same in every
// session. The attacker leaves Alice unstarted and replays Alice#2's flight
// to Bob, and Bob and Bob#2 both pass the checked ASSERT on a value Alice
// sent once. Every session once had to start, so both Alices sent deep. The
// checked comparison is pinned at one session.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private secret
	knows public tag
	deep = HASH(HASH(HASH(HASH(secret, tag), tag), tag), tag)
	shallow = HASH(secret, tag)
]

Alice -> Bob: deep, shallow

principal Bob[
	knows private secret
	knows public tag
	local_deep = HASH(HASH(HASH(HASH(secret, tag), tag), tag), tag)
	_ = ASSERT(local_deep, deep)?
	checked_use = HASH(deep)
	_ = ASSERT(local_deep, shallow)
	loose_use = HASH(shallow)
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: deep
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: shallow
]
